severity: high

Root cause: the POST is succeeding at the network level but the server is rejecting the message body, and the client is not reading the response. The usual rejections are the word filter, a missing room membership, or an empty text field.

What the user sees: their message flashes in from the optimistic render, then vanishes on the next poll.

Fix, read the response and act on it:

```js
const sent = await fetch('/api/chat/messages', { method: 'POST', headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' }, body: JSON.stringify(payload) });
const d = await sent.json().catch(() => ({}));
if (d && d.filtered) {
  setMessages(prev => prev.filter(m => m.id !== optimistic.id));
  setBlocked('That word is filtered.');
  return;
}
```

The general bug class here is an optimistic update with no rollback path.

```ts
export function debounce<T extends (...args: any[]) => void>(fn: T, ms: number) {
  let timer: ReturnType<typeof setTimeout> | null = null;
  const wrapped = (...args: Parameters<T>) => {
    if (timer) clearTimeout(timer);
    timer = setTimeout(() => { timer = null; fn(...args); }, ms);
  };
  wrapped.cancel = () => { if (timer) { clearTimeout(timer); timer = null; } };
  return wrapped;
}
```

The cancel method matters in React. Call it in a useEffect cleanup or a pending timer fires after the component unmounts and warns about setting state on an unmounted component.

Root cause: setState is asynchronous and batched. Reading the state variable straight after setting it gives you the value from the current render.

Broken:
```js
setCount(count + 1);
console.log(count);
```

Use the functional form when the next value depends on the previous one, and use the value you computed rather than reading state back:
```js
const next = count + 1;
setCount(next);
console.log(next);
```

This also fixes the classic bug where two updates in the same tick collapse into one, because `setCount(c => c + 1)` queues correctly while `setCount(count + 1)` does not.

```js
async function fetchRetry(url, options = {}, tries = 3, baseMs = 300) {
  let lastErr;
  for (let i = 0; i < tries; i++) {
    try {
      const r = await fetch(url, options);
      if (r.status >= 500 && i < tries - 1) throw new Error('server ' + r.status);
      return r;
    } catch (e) {
      lastErr = e;
      if (i === tries - 1) break;
      await new Promise(res => setTimeout(res, baseMs * Math.pow(2, i) + Math.random() * 100));
    }
  }
  throw lastErr;
}
```

It only retries 5xx and network failures, never 4xx, because retrying a bad request just repeats the same rejection. The random jitter stops many clients retrying in lockstep.

severity: medium

Root cause: the installed service worker is still controlling the page and serving cached responses. Registering a new one does not take over until the old one is released.

Fix, skip waiting and claim clients in public/uv-sw.js:

```js
self.addEventListener('install', () => { self.skipWaiting(); });
self.addEventListener('activate', (event) => { event.waitUntil(self.clients.claim()); });
```

To verify a fix in the browser, clear it fully first:
```js
const rs = await navigator.serviceWorker.getRegistrations();
for (const r of rs) await r.unregister();
const ks = await caches.keys();
for (const k of ks) await caches.delete(k);
```

Otherwise you will test the old bundle and think your fix failed.

Store the count together with the timestamp it started, and treat an expired window as zero on read. Do not run a scheduled reset job.

```js
async function getUsage(kv, u){
  try{
    const raw = kv ? await kv.get('ai_usage_' + u) : null;
    const j = raw ? JSON.parse(raw) : null;
    if(!j || !j.since || Date.now() - j.since > 86400000) return { used: 0, since: Date.now() };
    return { used: Number(j.used) || 0, since: Number(j.since) || Date.now() };
  }catch{ return { used: 0, since: Date.now() }; }
}
```

The window starts at the user's first request, so each user rolls independently and you never need a cron. Exempt staff before you check the limit, not after, or you waste a read.

Two bugs, both from missing awaits.

fetch returns a Promise, so .json() is being called on a Promise, which does not have that method. And .json() itself returns a Promise.

Fix:
```js
const r = await fetch('/api/users');
if (!r.ok) throw new Error('users ' + r.status);
const data = await r.json();
```

The ok check matters. fetch only rejects on network failure, so a 404 or 500 resolves normally and you end up parsing an error page as JSON.

Capture at the window level, batch, and send without blocking the page.

src/errorReport.ts
```ts
const seen = new Set<string>();

export function startErrorReporting() {
  const send = (kind: string, message: string, stack: string) => {
    const key = kind + message.slice(0, 120);
    if (seen.has(key)) return;
    seen.add(key);
    const body = JSON.stringify({
      kind,
      message: message.slice(0, 500),
      stack: stack.slice(0, 1500),
      route: location.pathname,
      user: localStorage.getItem('batprox-user') || ''
    });
    fetch('/api/errors', { method: 'POST', headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' }, body, keepalive: true }).catch(() => {});
  };
  window.addEventListener('error', e => send('error', e.message || '', e.error?.stack || ''));
  window.addEventListener('unhandledrejection', e => send('rejection', String(e.reason?.message || e.reason || ''), e.reason?.stack || ''));
}
```

The dedupe set is the important part. Without it a render loop will fire thousands of identical reports and fill your store. keepalive lets the report survive the page unloading.

```js
function deepClone(value, seen = new WeakMap()) {
  if (value === null || typeof value !== 'object') return value;
  if (seen.has(value)) return seen.get(value);
  if (value instanceof Date) return new Date(value.getTime());
  if (value instanceof RegExp) return new RegExp(value.source, value.flags);
  if (value instanceof Map) {
    const out = new Map();
    seen.set(value, out);
    for (const [k, v] of value) out.set(deepClone(k, seen), deepClone(v, seen));
    return out;
  }
  if (value instanceof Set) {
    const out = new Set();
    seen.set(value, out);
    for (const v of value) out.add(deepClone(v, seen));
    return out;
  }
  const out = Array.isArray(value) ? [] : Object.create(Object.getPrototypeOf(value));
  seen.set(value, out);
  for (const k of Reflect.ownKeys(value)) out[k] = deepClone(value[k], seen);
  return out;
}
```

The WeakMap handles circular references, which is what breaks naive clones. Time O(n) in the number of properties, space O(n).

Edge cases it does not cover: functions are returned by reference, and class instances keep their prototype but skip any constructor logic. If your data is plain JSON, structuredClone is simpler and faster.

```js
class LRUCache {
  constructor(capacity) {
    this.capacity = capacity;
    this.map = new Map();
  }

  get(key) {
    if (!this.map.has(key)) return undefined;
    const value = this.map.get(key);
    this.map.delete(key);
    this.map.set(key, value);
    return value;
  }

  set(key, value) {
    if (this.map.has(key)) this.map.delete(key);
    else if (this.map.size >= this.capacity) this.map.delete(this.map.keys().next().value);
    this.map.set(key, value);
  }
}
```

This leans on Map keeping insertion order. Deleting and re-setting a key moves it to the newest position, so the oldest entry is always the first key.

Both get and set are O(1). The subtle bug people hit is returning early in get without reordering, which silently turns it into a plain cache with no recency tracking.

Root cause: forEach ignores the promise each async callback returns, so the awaits inside never hold anything up. The log prints before a single write finishes, and any rejection becomes an unhandled promise rejection.

Sequential, if order matters or the store dislikes concurrency:

```js
async function save() {
  for (const i of items) await db.put(i);
  console.log('done');
}
```

Parallel, if the writes are independent:

```js
async function save() {
  await Promise.all(items.map(i => db.put(i)));
  console.log('done');
}
```

Use Promise.allSettled instead if one failure should not abandon the rest.

In development with React 18 StrictMode, effects mount, unmount and mount again on purpose, to surface effects that are not cleanup safe. It does not happen in a production build.

So it is usually revealing a real bug rather than being one. The fix is to make the effect idempotent and clean up after itself:

```js
useEffect(() => {
  const controller = new AbortController();
  fetch('/api/data', { signal: controller.signal })
    .then(r => r.json())
    .then(setData)
    .catch(() => {});
  return () => controller.abort();
}, []);
```

If it also runs twice in production, that is a different problem: check the dependency array for a value recreated every render, such as an object or an inline function.

Make the write conditional on the version you read, so a stale write fails instead of overwriting.

```js
async function updateRecord(db, id, patch) {
  for (let attempt = 0; attempt < 3; attempt++) {
    const row = await db.prepare('SELECT v, version FROM kvstore WHERE k = ?').bind(id).first();
    if (!row) return false;
    const next = { ...JSON.parse(row.v), ...patch };
    const res = await db.prepare('UPDATE kvstore SET v = ?, version = version + 1 WHERE k = ? AND version = ?')
      .bind(JSON.stringify(next), id, row.version).run();
    if (res.meta.changes > 0) return true;
  }
  return false;
}
```

This is optimistic concurrency. If another writer landed first the version no longer matches, changes is 0, and the loop retries with fresh data.

The alternative is doing the whole change in one SQL statement so there is no read-then-write gap at all, which is better when the update can be expressed in SQL.

```js
function flatten(arr, depth = Infinity) {
  const out = [];
  const stack = [[arr, depth]];
  while (stack.length) {
    const [current, d] = stack.pop();
    for (let i = current.length - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
      const item = current[i];
      if (Array.isArray(item) && d > 0) stack.push([item, d - 1]);
      else out.push(item);
    }
  }
  return out.reverse();
}
```

Iterative rather than recursive, so a deeply nested array cannot blow the call stack. Time O(n) in total elements, space O(n).

For ordinary depths `arr.flat(Infinity)` is built in and faster. Write this only when you need the depth guard or you are avoiding the stack limit.

severity: high

Two problems.

First, JSON.parse throws if the key is missing or holds invalid JSON, and the throw escapes to the request handler. Guard it.

Second, this is a read-modify-write with no protection. Two requests arriving together both read the same list and the later write erases the earlier one, so a user silently disappears.

```js
const data = await kv.get('users');
let users = [];
try { users = data ? JSON.parse(data) : []; } catch { users = []; }
if (!Array.isArray(users)) users = [];
users.push(newUser);
await kv.put('users', JSON.stringify(users));
```

That fixes the crash. The race still exists, so for anything where a lost write matters, do the append in a single D1 statement instead of reading the whole array into JavaScript.

```js
const RL_MAP = new Map();

function rl(key, limit, windowMs) {
  const now = Date.now();
  const entry = RL_MAP.get(key);
  if (!entry || now - entry.start > windowMs) {
    RL_MAP.set(key, { start: now, count: 1 });
    return true;
  }
  entry.count += 1;
  if (entry.count > limit) return false;
  return true;
}
```

This is a fixed window counter. O(1) per call.

Two honest limits. The Map lives in worker memory, so each isolate has its own counter and the real limit across a region is higher than the number you set. And a fixed window allows a burst of up to 2x the limit across a window boundary. For strict limits you need a sliding window and shared storage such as Durable Objects.

The Map also grows unbounded. Evict expired keys periodically or cap its size.

Use cursor pagination rather than offset. Offset re-scans rows and skips or repeats items when the list changes between pages.

```js
if(url.pathname==='/api/items' && request.method==='GET'){
  const h=cors(new Headers(), request.headers.get('Origin')); h.set('Content-Type','application/json');
  const limit=Math.max(1, Math.min(100, parseInt(url.searchParams.get('limit')||'25',10)||25));
  const cursor=Number(url.searchParams.get('cursor')||0);
  const raw=kv?await kv.get('items'):null;
  let all=[];
  try{ all=raw?JSON.parse(raw):[]; }catch{ all=[]; }
  if(!Array.isArray(all)) all=[];
  const page=all.filter(x=>!cursor||x.ts<cursor).sort((a,b)=>b.ts-a.ts).slice(0,limit);
  const next=page.length===limit?page[page.length-1].ts:null;
  return new Response(JSON.stringify({items:page, nextCursor:next}),{headers:h});
}
```

The cursor is the timestamp of the last item seen, so new arrivals never shift the page under the client. nextCursor being null means the end.

Remember to add /api/items to both arrays in functions/api/[[path]].ts or it returns 404 on the live site.

